 ],
 "literal": "With a sharp spear, wiping the sweat of his forehead, / the king speaks harsh demands. And the father, / other than haughty words, will not speak submissively. / If this is their nature, she of sharp teeth, / red-streaked, lustrous, cool eyes, the beautiful dark young woman, / like a small fire born in a tree, / has become a torment to the very town where she was born.",
 "translation": "Wiping the sweat from his forehead with the tip of his sharp spear, the king speaks harsh demands. The girl's father, too, speaks only haughty words and will never speak submissively. Since this is the nature of both sides, this beautiful, dark young woman with sharp teeth and red-streaked, lustrous, cool eyes has become like a small spark of fire born within a tree, bringing destruction to the very town where she was born.",
 "modern": "கூர்மையான வேலின் நுனியால் தன் நெற்றி வியர்வையைத் துடைத்துக் கொண்டு அரசன் சினந்து பேசுகிறான். பெண்ணின் தந்தையோ, பணிந்து பேசாமல் வீராப்பாகவே பேசுகிறான். இரு தரப்பினரின் நிலையும் இவ்வாறு இருக்க, கூர்மையான பற்களையும், செவ்வரி ஓடிய குளிர்ந்த கண்களையும் உடைய இந்த அழகிய இளம்பெண், மரத்தில் தோன்றும் சிறு நெருப்பு அந்த மரத்தையே அழிப்பது போல, தான் பிறந்த ஊருக்கே அழிவைத் தரும் அணங்காக மாறிவிட்டாள்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by a concerned bystander or town elder witnessing a tense standoff. A powerful king has arrived with his army to demand the hand of a local chieftain's daughter, wiping his sweating brow with his spear tip as he makes harsh demands. The girl's father, proud and unyielding, refuses to submit and speaks only defiant words. The speaker laments that because of the stubborn pride of both men, the beautiful young woman has become a source of doom for her own community. She is compared to a tiny spark of fire born inside a tree, which eventually burns down the entire tree that nurtured it. This poem belongs to the Kāñci tiṇai and the Makaṭpāṟ Kāñci tuṟai, which depicts the tragic consequences when a father refuses to give his daughter in marriage to a king.",
